flour-note-rye = From about 20 % rye, sourdough or an acidifier gives a more elastic crumb that slices better.
flour-note-additions = Seeds, bran and grains above 15 % of the flour: beyond the range these coefficients were measured on.
flour-note-psyllium = Psyllium above 5 % of the flour: beyond the tested range.
flour-note-scald = More than 40 % of the flour scalded: beyond the tested range.
flour-note-protein = { $flour }: this protein is more than 3 points from the type's usual value; the correction stops at 3 points.
flour-note-no-data = { $flour }: no usual bread hydration is known for this flour.
flour-note-no-absorption = { $flour }: no absorption value is known for this flour, so your recipe's hydration cannot be carried over.
flour-note-target = The blend already has this protein: no vital gluten needed.
flour-how = Each flour starts from the usual bread hydration of its type, moved by one point per point of protein when you give the bag's value (Tipples, 1978). The blend is the weighted mean of its flours. If you give a recipe that already works, its hydration is kept instead, and only the difference in water absorption between its flour and your blend is added. Each addition brings the water it takes at equal dough consistency, and a scald adds water in proportion to the flour scalded. Standards fix the ash of a flour type and little else, so about half of the values per type are estimates: take the result as a starting point, hold some water back, and write down what your own flour takes.
